# 12. Check Linux Logs

Even without an installed Wazuh Agent, you can understand the data Wazuh would monitor.

Authentication logs:

```bash
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/auth.log
```

SSH events:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u ssh --since today
```

System events:

```bash
sudo journalctl --since today
```

---

# 13. Create a Wazuh-Style Investigation

Use the same investigation mindset from Day 22.

Look for failed SSH attempts:

```bash
sudo grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log
```

Count them:

```bash
sudo grep -c "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log
```

Successful logins:

```bash
sudo grep "Accepted" /var/log/auth.log
```

Possible sudo activity:

```bash
sudo grep "sudo" /var/log/auth.log
```

Now imagine these events are being collected centrally by Wazuh.

---

# 14. Example Wazuh Investigation

Suppose your dashboard shows:

```text
Alert:
Multiple SSH Authentication Failures

Host:
ubuntu-server

User:
admin

Source IP:
192.168.1.50

Severity:
High
```

Don't immediately conclude:

```text
Attacker!
```

Instead investigate:

```text
Who?
What?
When?
Where?
Why?
```

---

# 15. Correlate the Alert

Search for other events from the same source IP.

```text
192.168.1.50
      ↓
Failed SSH
      ↓
Failed SSH
      ↓
Failed SSH
      ↓
Successful SSH
      ↓
sudo
      ↓
New Process
      ↓
Network Connection
```

Now the alert has context.

This is exactly what you practiced on Days 20–22.

